      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "Contextual uncertainty in the primate locus coeruleus: Reconciling theory, behavior and physiology through a combined approach"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "pdfua_tag": "P",
      "id": 135,
      "page number": 1,
      "bounding box": [72.0, 707.778, 378.085, 716.832],
      "font": "TimesNewRoman",
      "font size": 9.96,
      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "Azriel Ghadooshahy, Ritwik K. Niyogi, Sheeraz Ahmad, Robert Desimone"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "pdfua_tag": "H1",
      "id": 136,
      "level": "Subtitle",
      "page number": 1,
      "bounding box": [72.0, 682.5, 128.634, 693.204],
      "heading level": 1,
      "font": "TimesNewRoman,Bold",
      "font size": 12.0,
      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "Summary"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "pdfua_tag": "P",
      "id": 137,
      "page number": 1,
      "bounding box": [72.0, 585.9, 542.994, 679.608],
      "font": "TimesNewRoman",
      "font size": 12.0,
      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "Mounting theoretical and experimental evidence suggests that major ascending modulatory systems including dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin and acetylcholine play specific computational roles in neural processing beyond their classically attributed roles in mediating non-specific arousal. Indeed, recent computational work has linked activity in the locus coeruleus-norepinephrine (LC-NE) system to statistical properties of the environment in the form of unexpected uncertainty at two distinct timescales (Yu and Dayan, 2005 Dayan and Yu, 2006)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "pdfua_tag": "P",
      "id": 138,
      "page number": 1,
      "bounding box": [72.0, 434.1, 542.994, 583.008],
      "font": "TimesNewRoman",
      "font size": 12.0,
      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "A key feature—and challenge—of modeling the LC-NE system with Bayesian theory is to take into account the observer’s (subjective) beliefs about behavioral context and task demands. Thus, our approach is to construct generative models of behavioral strategy from choice-behavior, reaction time data and pupil diameter measurements that allow us to quantitatively test model-driven hypothesis about task-related LC neuronal activity. We believe that this approach will enable an unprecedented degree of interpretive power and specificity in studying the LC-NE system and its role in cognitive-behavioral control. Moreover, this work is likely to clarify the relationship between LC-NE activity and non-luminance-mediated pupil diameter modulations, which provide a valuable link to human studies and may to be useful as a non-invasive measurement of otherwise hidden internal states related to arousal and cognitive control."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "pdfua_tag": "P",
      "id": 139,
      "page number": 1,
      "bounding box": [71.994, 365.1, 542.994, 431.208],
      "font": "TimesNewRoman",
      "font size": 12.0,
      "text color": "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content": "Here we present data from preliminary experimental and modeling efforts. A promising candidate model of behavioral strategy qualitatively captures the biasing effects of contextual priors on choice-behavior and reaction times. In addition, preliminary pupillometric and putative LC recording data reveal complex effects of both withinand between-trial contextual uncertainty."
    },
    {
